SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 315 | Thursday, March 19, 2026

Good morning.

1) The largest LTL carrier: tonnage per day up 2.1% in Q1 to date
First positive quarter in nine. The CEO called it "the early innings of an industrial recovery" and then spent the rest of the call lowering the bar for Q2. Yields rose 6%; the LTL industry has priced its way through the downturn and intends to keep the discipline on the way up.

2) The USMCA joint review is fourteen weeks out and the lobbying has started
Automakers want the rules-of-origin thresholds left alone; steel wants them raised; the agricultural groups want dairy access to Canada on the table. The review can end with the agreement extended, renegotiated, or put on a one-year clock. Bet on the clock.

3) The FAA finalizes beyond-visual-line-of-sight rules for delivery drones
Operators no longer need a case-by-case waiver for routine flights under 400 feet in approved corridors. Two rural pharmacy chains and one big-box retailer announced expansions within the hour. For freight this is a rounding error; for the last mile in low-density areas it is a real cost curve change.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 38 hours. Median time from PO to confirmed ship date at mid-market manufacturers with automated order promising, versus 4.5 days at those without, per a benchmarking report out today.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

